t appropriated for that purpose but  here the Trump administration will  counter that Congress has in fact passed  a law that gives him authority to do  this a number of laws do in fact  authorize the President to take certain  actions by declaring a national  emergency the primary law that the  president seems to be operating under is  the national emergencies act of 1976 as  amended in 1985 which governs the  president's declaration of a national  emergency now under the NEA the  president must specifically state the  law under which he proposes to act and  he still has to comply with a provision  of that law this is incredibly important  because the re-designated funds must  still comply with the original law under  which the funds were appropriated the  first preliminary issue is can the  president even invoke the NEA in these  circumstances the question is is there  an emergency and it also depends on what  the definition of an emergency is the  term emergency is not defined in the  national emergencies act and this fact  cuts in a couple of different directions  on the one hand some argue that this  gives the president carte blanche to  declare anything an emergency because  the NEA doesn't specify what constitutes  an emergency but I think that misses the  point one of the jobs of the courts from  time immemorial is to provide  definitions where a particular statute  is silent or ambiguous in the case of  federal laws courts often look to the  intent of Congress to impute a  definition to an otherwise undefined  term and let's be clear there is no  emergency as most people understand that  word Merriam Webster's defines emergency  has a unforeseen combination of  circumstances or the resulting state  that calls for immediate action  president Trump has said himself that  this was not strictly necessary he could  have gone through regular channels but  he wanted to do it faster I want to do  it faster I could do the wall over a  longer period of time I didn't need to  do this but I'd rather do it much faster  and in the present day illegal  immigration is at near historic lows the  President may not be satisfied with  those numbers but as most people  understand the lay definition of  emergency it doesn't seem like there's  any argument that it fits it seems very  likely that this is not the kind of  situation that Congress had in mind when  passing the 1976 national emergencies  act in fact that particular law was an  attempt to curtail presidential power  that had been exercised by the Nixon  administration so it is possible that  the court will defer to whatever the  president declares as a national  emergency I don't think the courts that  look at this will do so in part because  of the political ramifications that  might allow future presidents to declare  whatever they want as an emergency but  that being said  it isn't uncommon for courts to have to  weigh in on the definitions of terms in  various laws and if the court uses a  layer of emergency then it's likely that  this particular situation would not  qualify and that may end the analysis of  this particular legal question right  there but let's assume for the sake of  argument that this does qualify as a  national emergency or the court takes a  deferential view that says basically  anything can be a national emergency  well now that means that we have to look  at the second part what laws are invoked  in order to re-appropriate the funds  because as I mentioned before the  president still has to comply with the  underlying laws that pass the funds in  the first place one of the laws that the  president is relying on to receive the  lion's share of the billions of dollars  he's asking for border wall construction  deals with military construction under  this military construction law 33 USC  2293 the president may turn eighth or  defer military construction that he has  deemed non-essential to the National  Defense and may apply those resources  for a project to construct another  project which he considers essential but  the reappropriation funds under this  particular law must still constitute  military construction and given the fact  that we are not at war with Mexico it  makes it a hard sell to claim that a  border wall is a military construction  project in addition this particular  construction project does not require  the Armed Forces to build and in fact  there is an argument to be made that the  military cannot be used to build this  particular project under the Posse  Comitatus rule Posse Comitatus is a  doctrine that is enshrined in statutory  law in the United States it says you  cannot use the military for domestic  projects especially not enforcement you  might recall that when President Trump  sent troops to the border to fight  against an alleged caravan of potential  immigrants and refugees they weren't  actually sent to the border to fight but  really to only provide backup services  to the department of homeland security  that was largely because of the doctrine  of Posse Comitatus which says that you  cannot use Armed Forces domestically so  even if the press  and is able to get over the hurdle of  declaring a national emergency he's  still going to have a number of hurdles  with respect to the underlying law from  which he is taking the appropriated  funds now on the other hand the  government will probably argue that the  courts should give broad deference to  the president's determination that there  is a national emergency and also broad  deference as to what constitutes  military construction as the  commander-in-chief of the Armed Forces  we don't often want to be able to  second-guess the commander-in-chief's  ability to declare what is essential to  the national defense and what isn't so  if this case gets to the Supreme Court  and isn't bounced on some technicality  it's completely unclear which way the  Supreme Court might go on this there are  long strings of cases that are  deferential 